      "doi": "10.4271/780199",
      "publicationDate": "1978-02-01",
      "title": "Water-Gas Shift and Steam Reforming Reactions Over a Rhodium Three-Way Catalyst",
      "type": "article",
      "url": "https://openalex.org/works/W1584534578",
      "abstract": "Under oxygen-deficient (rich) conditions, a potential route to maintaining control of carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrocarbon (HC) emissions in a three-way system is through reactions of these pollutants with water vapor (H 2 O). The importance of such reactions over supported rhodium was investigated in the laboratory. The water-gas shift (CO + H 2 O) was insignificant. Steam reforming (HC + H 2 O) took place, but gave CO as a product. Thus, if CO conversion governs the rich-side effectiveness of a three-way catalyst, steam reforming which converts HC to CO is unlikely, in itself, to improve three-way performance.",
